,"robust","inner maximization","outer minimization","Inner maximization","Outer minimization","instruct","adversarial training","Paraphrase","Retokenization"],"orig_elements":"","link_texts":["#","Morris et al. 2020","Ribeiro et al (2018)","EDA","Wei & Zou 2019","Jin et al. 2019","Li et al. 2020","#","Guo et al. 2021","Jang et al. 2016","Zhang et al. 2019","Ebrahimi et al. 2018","Wallace et al. (2019)","HotFlip","Shin et al., 2020","Mehrabi et al. (2022)","Zou et al. (2023)","OpenAI 2023","UAT and AutoPrompt","Jones et al. 2023","#","Wei et al. (2023)","DAN","AIM","it can understand","Wei et al. (2023)","Greshake et al. (2023)","#","Wallace et al. (2019)","QuizBowl QA dataset","Ziegler et al. (2022)","Wallace et al. (2019)","BERT-Attack","Xu et al. 2021","BAD dataset","Tensorflow dataset","red-teaming dataset","Ganguli, et al. 2022","GPT-4","DALL-E 3","#","Perez et al. 2022","A2C","BAD (Bot Adversarial Dialogue) dataset","nucleus sampling","Casper et al. (2023)","Perez et al. (2022)","here","here","here","Mehrabi et al. 2023","in-context learning","Perez et al. 2022","Schramowski et al. 2022","https://github.com/notAI-tech/NudeNet)","Perspective API","#","#","Madry et al. 2017","#","Xie et al. 2023","Jain et al. 2023","White-box attacks","UAT-LM","Jain et al. 2023","#","#","“Towards Deep Learning Models Resistant to Adversarial Attacks”","“Semantically equivalent adversarial rules for debugging NLP models”","“Gradient-based adversarial attacks against text transformers”","“HotFlip: White-Box Adversarial Examples for Text Classification”","“Universal Adversarial Triggers for Attacking and Analyzing NLP.”","code","“Robust Conversational Agents against Imperceptible Toxicity Triggers.”","“Universal and Transferable Adversarial Attacks on Aligned Language Models.”","“RLPrompt: Optimizing Discrete Text Prompts with Reinforcement Learning.”","“Is BERT Really Robust? A Strong Baseline for Natural Language Attack on Text Classification and Entailment.”","“BERT-Attack: Adversarial Attack Against BERT Using BERT.”","\"TextAttack: A Framework for Adversarial Attacks, Data Augmentation, and Adversarial Training in NLP.\"","“Bot-Adversarial Dialogue for Safe Conversational Agents.”","“Adversarial training for high-stakes reliability.”","“Red Teaming Language Models to Reduce Harms: Methods, Scaling Behaviors, and Lessons Learned.”","“Red Teaming Language Models with Language Models.”","“Red Teaming Language Models to Reduce Harms: Methods, Scaling Behaviors, and Lessons Learned.”","“FLIRT: Feedback Loop In-context Red Teaming.”","“Explore, Establish, Exploit: Red Teaming Language Models from Scratch.”","“Defending ChatGPT against Jailbreak Attack via Self-Reminder.”","“Automatically Auditing Large Language Models via Discrete Optimization.”","“Compromising Real-World LLM-Integrated Applications with Indirect Prompt Injection.”","“Baseline Defenses for Adversarial Attacks Against Aligned Language Models.”","“Jailbroken: How Does LLM Safety Training Fail?”","“EDA: Easy data augmentation techniques for boosting performance on text classification tasks.”","www.jailbreakchat.com","“You can use GPT-4 to create prompt injections against GPT-4”","Nlp","Language-Model","Safety","Adversarial Attacks","Robustness","Redteam"],"chroma:document":"Sample test inputs from a red-teamer LLM $\\mathbf{x} \\sim p_\\text{red}(.)$.\n\nUse the target LLM $p(\\mathbf{y} \\mid \\mathbf{x})$ to generate an output $\\mathbf{y}$ for each test case $\\mathbf{x}$.\n\nIdentify a subset of test cases leading to harmful output according to the classifier $r(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y})$.\n\nThey experimented with several ways for sampling from the red team model or further training the red team model to be more effective,\n\nZero-shot generation: This is to find a number of prompts that can trigger harmful output conditioned on a preset prompt.\n\nStochastic few-shot generation: The red team prompts found from the above step are then used as few-shot examples to generate more similar cases. Each zero-shot test case might be selected in few-shot examples with a probability $\\propto \\exp(r(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) / \\tau)$"}␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀