al. (2023)","Greshake et al. (2023)","#","Wallace et al. (2019)","QuizBowl QA dataset","Ziegler et al. (2022)","Wallace et al. (2019)","BERT-Attack","Xu et al. 2021","BAD dataset","Tensorflow dataset","red-teaming dataset","Ganguli, et al. 2022","GPT-4","DALL-E 3","#","Perez et al. 2022","A2C","BAD (Bot Adversarial Dialogue) dataset","nucleus sampling","Casper et al. (2023)","Perez et al. (2022)","here","here","here","Mehrabi et al. 2023","in-context learning","Perez et al. 2022","Schramowski et al. 2022","https://github.com/notAI-tech/NudeNet)","Perspective API","#","#","Madry et al. 2017","#","Xie et al. 2023","Jain et al. 2023","White-box attacks","UAT-LM","Jain et al. 2023","#","#","“Towards Deep Learning Models Resistant to Adversarial Attacks”","“Semantically equivalent adversarial rules for debugging NLP models”","“Gradient-based adversarial attacks against text transformers”","“HotFlip: White-Box Adversarial Examples for Text Classification”","“Universal Adversarial Triggers for Attacking and Analyzing NLP.”","code","“Robust Conversational Agents against Imperceptible Toxicity Triggers.”","“Universal and Transferable Adversarial Attacks on Aligned Language Models.”","“RLPrompt: Optimizing Discrete Text Prompts with Reinforcement Learning.”","“Is BERT Really Robust? A Strong Baseline for Natural Language Attack on Text Classification and Entailment.”","“BERT-Attack: Adversarial Attack Against BERT Using BERT.”","\"TextAttack: A Framework for Adversarial Attacks, Data Augmentation, and Adversarial Training in NLP.\"","“Bot-Adversarial Dialogue for Safe Conversational Agents.”","“Adversarial training for high-stakes reliability.”","“Red Teaming Language Models to Reduce Harms: Methods, Scaling Behaviors, and Lessons Learned.”","“Red Teaming Language Models with Language Models.”","“Red Teaming Language Models to Reduce Harms: Methods, Scaling Behaviors, and Lessons Learned.”","“FLIRT: Feedback Loop In-context Red Teaming.”","“Explore, Establish, Exploit: Red Teaming Language Models from Scratch.”","“Defending ChatGPT against Jailbreak Attack via Self-Reminder.”","“Automatically Auditing Large Language Models via Discrete Optimization.”","“Compromising Real-World LLM-Integrated Applications with Indirect Prompt Injection.”","“Baseline Defenses for Adversarial Attacks Against Aligned Language Models.”","“Jailbroken: How Does LLM Safety Training Fail?”","“EDA: Easy data augmentation techniques for boosting performance on text classification tasks.”","www.jailbreakchat.com","“You can use GPT-4 to create prompt injections against GPT-4”","Nlp","Language-Model","Safety","Adversarial Attacks","Robustness","Redteam"],"filetype":"text/html","category":"CompositeElement","element_id":"2775507ceeaaa257529304164be2b816","orig_elements":"","url":"https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2023-10-25-adv-attack-llm/","emphasized_text_tags":["b","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","b","i","i","i","b","b","b","b","b","b","i","b","b","i","b","b","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","b","i","b","b","i","b","i","i","i","b","b","i","i","i","i","i","b","i","i"],"chroma:document":"In each FLIRT iteration,\n\nThe red LM $p_\\text{red}$ generates an adversarial prompt $\\mathbf{x} \\sim p_\\text{red}(. \\mid {\\small{\\text{examples}}})$; The initial in-context examples are handcrafted by human;\n\nThe generative model $p$ generates an image or a text output $\\mathbf{y}$ conditioned on this prompt $\\mathbf{y} \\sim p(.\\mid \\mathbf{x})$;\n\nThe generated content $\\mathbf{y}$ is evaluated whether it is safety using e.g. classifiers;\n\nIf it is deemed unsafe, the trigger prompt $\\mathbf{x}$ is used to update in-context exemplars for $p_\\text{red}$ to generate new adversarial prompts according to a strategy.\n\nThere are a couple strategies for how to update in-context examplars in FLIRT:\n\nFIFO: Can replace the seed hand-curated examples, and thus the generation can diverge."}␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀